--TEST--
An `int` converts into an enum only where a case names that value, and throws naming every case otherwise — `rule:types/conversion`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: lang:enums/from-an-integer-back-to-a-case
enum Mode {
    Read,
    Write,
    Exec = 4,
}

class Gate {
    // `rule:types/conversion`'s second row. The conversion itself is free — an enum is
    // a tag over its backing integer — so what the row costs is the check in
    // front of it, and that check is against every case of the *declaration*
    // rather than a subset named at the site. That is what keeps the enum a
    // closed set once a plain integer can reach it.
    public static function of(int $n): string {
        try {
            Mode $m = $n as Mode;
            return "case " . (($m as int) as string);
        } catch (Throwable $e) {
            return "threw: " . $e->message;
        }
    }
}

echo Gate::of(0), "\n";
echo Gate::of(1), "\n";
echo Gate::of(4), "\n";
echo Gate::of(2), "\n";
echo Gate::of(-1), "\n";

// An operand already at the enum's own representation names a case by
// construction — two different enums are never interconvertible, so this is
// the same enum — and pays no comparison at all.
Mode $held = Mode::Write;
echo ($held as Mode) as int, "\n";
--EXPECT--
case 0
case 1
case 4
threw: `2` is not one of `Mode::Read`, `Mode::Write`, `Mode::Exec`
threw: `-1` is not one of `Mode::Read`, `Mode::Write`, `Mode::Exec`
1
